    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The constitution of 1886, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-nunez">Rafael Núñez</a>, made Colombia a centralised Catholic republic and lasted, much amended, until 1991.</li><li>The republic lost Panama in 1903 and lived through La Violencia and a long armed conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, paramilitaries and cartels.</li><li>The constitution of 1991 and the 2016 peace agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutionary-armed-forces-of-colombia">FARC</a> reshaped a country that now has about 53 million people.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-nunez">Rafael Núñez</a>, the president behind the constitution of 1886, also wrote the words of Colombia’s national anthem, “¡Oh gloria inmarcesible!”, made official in 1920.</p></aside>
    </section>

